---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Work';
const description =
  'One exhibit, not hidden: this website. Built the way we build client sites, scored by our own public checker, source inspectable. Plus the teardown of what stood here before.';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'Work',
  description,
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://snippettester.com/#website' },
};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} schema={[schema]}>
  <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Work</p>
      <h1>One exhibit. Here is why it is not hidden behind a stock photo grid.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This practice is new, so the portfolio is one item long. Padding it with mockups, unbuilt
        concepts or client logos from other people's projects would be the ordinary move and it would
        undermine the only thing being sold here. So: one real site, measurable, with the source open.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top: 0;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card card--flag" style="max-width: 52rem;">
        <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Exhibit 01 · live · source inspectable</p>
        <h2 style="font-size: 1.6rem;">This website</h2>
        <p>
          Built exactly the way a client build is built — static HTML from Astro, schema on every
          page, answer-first copy, no web fonts, no analytics, no cookie banner. Its homepage scores 91 on the
          checker linked in its own header, and no page here scores below 76. You can run that check yourself right now, which is the
          entire point of publishing the tool.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="table-scroll" style="max-width: 52rem;">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">This site</th><th scope="col">Typical small business site</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">AEO checker score</th><td class="num">91/100</td><td class="num">40-65</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Largest page, HTML</th><td class="num">24 KB</td><td class="num">80-250 KB</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">CSS</th><td class="num">10 KB</td><td class="num">150-600 KB</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Web fonts</th><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">2-6</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">JavaScript required to read content</th><td>None</td><td>Often all of it</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Third-party trackers</th><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">4-20</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Cookie consent flow</th><td>Not needed</td><td>Required</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Monthly hosting</th><td class="num">$0</td><td class="num">$15-90</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="source-note" style="max-width: 52rem;">
        Comparison figures are typical ranges rather than a controlled study — treat them as
        indicative. The left column is measurable: run
        <a href="/#checker">the checker</a> against any page here, or read the source. Page weight
        matters because
        <a href="https://www.wislr.com/articles/ai-bot-behavior-log-analysis" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">AI crawlers fetch JavaScript as text without executing it</a>,
        so anything rendered client-side is invisible to them regardless of how it looks in a browser.
      </p>

      <div class="prose" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <h2>Exhibit 02: the before</h2>
        <p>
          The same domain used to host a failed SaaS product. Rather than quietly deleting it, the
          old landing page was audited in public and the findings published — two H1s, no structured
          data despite a full FAQ section, zero tables, one statistic on the entire page, and eleven
          links pointing at nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is the most useful thing in this portfolio, because it is a documented before and after
          on a single domain, and because the subject cannot complain.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/teardowns/auditing-our-own-abandoned-product">Read teardown 01 →</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout" style="max-width: 52rem;">
        <p>
          <strong>Wanted: the first three client builds.</strong> New practice, thin portfolio, and
          the honest way to fix that is to trade. The first three builds go at a reduced rate in
          exchange for permission to publish the whole thing — the before score, the decisions, the
          after score, and anything that went wrong.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
          <a class="btn btn--accent" href="/contact?first-build=1">Ask about a first build</a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        <h2>What is deliberately missing</h2>
        <ul class="quiet-list">
          <li>Client logos. There are no clients yet, and borrowed logos from freelance work elsewhere would be dishonest here.</li>
          <li>Design mockups. Anyone can produce a beautiful image of a site that was never built and never measured.</li>
          <li>Testimonials. Same reason.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This page gets longer as work gets done. Until then it is short, and the shortness is
          accurate information about how new this is.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
